READ FIRST (binding): CLAUDE.md; STRUCTURE.md (§2 core files, §7 naming/API routes, 400-line cap); docs/architecture.md sections "D-013 — Idempotency keys (reservation, atomic completion via route capture)" and "D-014 — API envelope (keyset pagination + error shape + DB-guard error translation)" COMPLETELY (normative — implement EXACTLY), plus skim D-007 (tenancy), D-010 (audit), D-011 (run_in_uow), D-025 (tests). Then read existing code: backend/app/core/{db.py,deps.py,schemas.py,exceptions.py,models.py,events.py,tenancy.py,numbering.py}, backend/app/main.py, backend/app/core/security_router.py, backend/app/core/docflow_router.py, backend/tests/conftest.py.

TASK — PLAN 3.8 remaining halves: idempotency (D-013) + cursor pagination (D-014).

PART A — Idempotency (D-013), `backend/app/core/idempotency.py`:
- `core_idempotency_keys` model (table `core_idempotency_keys`): per D-013 PK is composite (tenant_id, endpoint, key). Columns: tenant_id, endpoint (str — a stable route identifier), key (str — the client-supplied Idempotency-Key header value), status (str: IN_PROGRESS|COMPLETED), request_hash (str — hash of the request body so a replay with a DIFFERENT body is rejected), response_status (int nullable), response_body (JSON nullable — the stored response to replay), created_at, completed_at (nullable). Composite PK or UuidPKMixin + UNIQUE(tenant_id, endpoint, key) — D-013 says PK(tenant, endpoint, key); implement that composite PK (note: TenantMixin still applies for the tenant filter; reconcile composite PK with the mixins — you may use UuidPKMixin + a UNIQUE(tenant,endpoint,key) if a composite natural PK fights the tenant_fk/UuidPK conventions, but PREFER the natural composite PK per D-013 and note the choice).
- The flow per D-013 (reservation + atomic completion via route capture):
  * `reserve(tenant_id, endpoint, key, request_hash)` — in a SEPARATE committed transaction (its own session/connection), INSERT a row with status IN_PROGRESS; if the PK already exists: load it — if COMPLETED and request_hash matches → return a "replay" signal carrying the stored response; if COMPLETED and hash differs → raise ConflictError ("idempotency.key_reused" 409); if IN_PROGRESS → raise ConflictError ("idempotency.in_progress" 409, a concurrent duplicate is still running). The separate-transaction insert is what makes two concurrent duplicates collide on the PK. 
  * `IdempotencyContext` with a `capture(response_status, response_body)` method that STAGES the completion UPDATE (status=COMPLETED, response_status, response_body, completed_at) onto the BUSINESS session — so the idempotency record and the business document commit ATOMICALLY in the same run_in_uow transaction. Per D-013: fail-closed if capture is forgotten (if the handler completes without calling capture, the IN_PROGRESS row should be cleaned up / not left dangling — D-013's stance: implement exactly — likely the dependency finalizer deletes the IN_PROGRESS reservation if capture wasn't called, OR marks it so it can be retried; FOLLOW D-013's text).
- A FastAPI dependency `idempotency_dependency` / `Idempotent` per D-013: reads the `Idempotency-Key` header (required on the endpoints that use this dep — missing header → 400 "idempotency.key_required"), computes the request_hash, calls reserve, and either short-circuits the request by returning the replayed response OR yields an IdempotencyContext the handler uses to capture. Design so a router does: `idem: IdempotencyContext = Depends(Idempotent("finance.journal.post"))` then after creating the document calls `idem.capture(...)` inside the same uow. Make the replay short-circuit work within FastAPI (if a completed key is replayed, return the stored response without running the handler body — a dependency can raise a special "replay" that an exception handler turns into the stored response, OR the dependency returns a sentinel the handler checks; choose the cleanest FastAPI-idiomatic approach that still lets capture be atomic — document the mechanism).
- Which endpoints require it: D-013 says every endpoint that creates a financial or stock document. None exist yet (finance is PLAN 4). So PROVIDE the infrastructure + a demonstration: add a TEST-ONLY example route on an in-test create_app() (like the rbac/audit tests do) that uses Idempotent + capture against a real audited model (e.g. creates a TenantSetting) to prove the full flow. Do NOT add a fake business endpoint to production app/.
- Migration: add `core_idempotency_keys` to a new revision `backend/alembic/versions/0007_idempotency.py` (revision 0007, down_revision 0006).

PART B — Cursor pagination (D-014), in `backend/app/core/pagination.py` (NEW core file — note it's a justified addition: pagination mechanics are cross-cutting; STRUCTURE §2 lists schemas.py for the envelope but the keyset MECHANICS warrant their own file; record like prior core additions, OR if you can keep it small put helpers in schemas.py — D-014 implies non-trivial cursor encoding so a dedicated pagination.py is justified; note your choice):
- Per D-014: keyset (seek) pagination, NEVER OFFSET. A `paginate(session, select_stmt, *, order_by, cursor, limit) -> Page[...]` helper (or a class) that:
  * encodes the cursor as base64url of the last row's sort-key values (+ a fingerprint of the sort+filter so a cursor from one query can't be misused on another → on mismatch raise a 400 "pagination.invalid_cursor");
  * builds the seek predicate (WHERE (sort_col1, sort_col2, id) > (last1, last2, lastid) for ascending; handle the tuple-comparison portably across Postgres and SQLite — SQLite supports row-value comparisons in modern versions; verify, else expand the predicate into the portable OR-form (a > x) OR (a = x AND b > y) ...);
  * always includes a unique tiebreaker (the PK/id) in the sort so the cursor is stable;
  * returns Page[T] {items, next_cursor (null when no more), limit} matching the existing core/schemas.py Page envelope.
- Provide a clean way for routers to use it (e.g. a CursorParams dependency parsing ?cursor=&limit= with a max limit cap, default limit e.g. 50, max e.g. 200).
- Verify the existing Page[T] in schemas.py matches; extend if needed (it currently has items/next_cursor/limit).

TESTS:
`backend/tests/core/test_idempotency.py`:
- first request with a key reserves + completes + returns response; the key row is COMPLETED with stored response;
- replay with same key + same body returns the SAME stored response WITHOUT re-running the handler (prove the side effect happened once — e.g. only one TenantSetting created);
- replay with same key + DIFFERENT body → 409 idempotency.key_reused;
- concurrent duplicate (simulate: reserve twice before completing) → second gets 409 idempotency.in_progress;
- missing Idempotency-Key header on a guarded route → 400 idempotency.key_required;
- atomicity: if the business work fails after reserve, the IN_PROGRESS reservation does not get left as COMPLETED (and the business doc isn't created) — verify the fail-closed cleanup per D-013;
- tenant isolation: same key under two tenants are independent.
`backend/tests/core/test_pagination.py`:
- paginate returns first page of N, next_cursor set; following the cursor returns the next page with no overlap and no gaps; last page has next_cursor null;
- a tampered/foreign cursor (wrong fingerprint) → 400 pagination.invalid_cursor;
- stable under a tiebreaker (rows with equal sort key are not skipped/duplicated) — create rows with duplicate sort values and page through proving all are returned exactly once;
- limit cap enforced (?limit over max clamped);
- ordering correctness (ascending/descending if you support both).
Use a real tenant-scoped model for both (TenantSetting / User) on the migrated template DB.

RULES: no TODO/stub/placeholder; absolute imports; ruff + pytest FULLY green; delete stray atlas.db; files <400 lines; comments only for non-obvious constraints; don't print file contents in final reply. CRITICAL: verify row-value tuple comparison works on the project's aiosqlite; if not, use the expanded OR-form and document it. The idempotency replay short-circuit + atomic capture is subtle — make the tests unambiguous that the side effect runs exactly once.

Return: files created/changed, test counts (total + new), the public APIs (Idempotent dependency usage, paginate signature, CursorParams), whether row-value comparison worked on SQLite (y/n + fallback), any new core file recorded, and every deviation with one-line justification. Note any D-013/D-014 element deferred and to which PLAN task.

